1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,189
This video runs the full pipeline on three more regular expressions. Each one has a larger alphabet.

2
00:00:07,639 --> 00:00:21,933
The first is the Under Pressure machine from the course notes. The other two are the questions in questions-pre 4.8. They have no posted solution, so we solve them ourselves and check every answer in python.

3
00:00:22,383 --> 00:00:36,889
The course notes call this the Under Pressure machine. The song is a duet. The pair d b stands for David Bowie, and the pair f m stands for Freddie Mercury. The regex is d b or f m, star.

4
00:00:37,339 --> 00:00:40,582
The empty string matches: zero repetitions.

5
00:00:41,032 --> 00:00:43,379
d b matches: one repetition.

6
00:00:43,829 --> 00:00:46,901
f m d b matches: two repetitions.

7
00:00:47,351 --> 00:00:49,463
Three repetitions also match.

8
00:00:49,913 --> 00:00:53,475
d alone does not match. A pair is not finished.

9
00:00:53,925 --> 00:00:58,192
b d does not match. The letters of a pair have a fixed order.

10
00:00:58,642 --> 00:01:02,674
d b f does not match. The last pair is not finished.

11
00:01:03,124 --> 00:01:11,017
d m does not match. d must be followed by b. A python check with the regex gave all eight answers.

12
00:01:11,467 --> 00:01:22,027
Here is the N F A from the course notes. Ten states. State 1 is the start, and state 7 accepts. Dashed purple edges are epsilon edges.

13
00:01:22,477 --> 00:01:31,096
In blue, the two concatenations: d then b, and f then m. Each is two constant machines joined end to end.

14
00:01:31,546 --> 00:01:40,485
In orange, the alternation template. State 2 is the new entry, and state 6 is the new exit. Four epsilon edges.

15
00:01:40,935 --> 00:01:56,231
In purple, the repetition template. State 1 is the new entry and state 7 the new exit. 1 to 2 goes in, 6 to 7 goes out, 1 to 7 skips, and 6 back to 2 repeats. Every part matches a template.

16
00:01:56,681 --> 00:02:13,278
Step 2. The start state P is the closure of 1. From 1 we reach 2 and 7. From 2 we reach 3 and 8. So P is 1, 2, 3, 7, 8. It contains 7, so P accepts. The empty string is in the language.

17
00:02:13,728 --> 00:02:22,944
P on d. Only 3 has a d edge, to 4. State 4 has no epsilon edges. So Q is the set with only 4.

18
00:02:23,394 --> 00:02:29,730
P on f. Only 8 has an f edge, to 9. So R is the set with only 9.

19
00:02:30,180 --> 00:02:40,292
P on b and P on m. No state in P has those edges. Both go to the empty set, X. A pair cannot start with b or m.

20
00:02:40,742 --> 00:02:58,790
Q on b. 4 goes to 5. Close: 5 reaches 6, and 6 reaches 2 and 7. Then 2 reaches 3 and 8. The set is 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8. Call it S. It contains 7, so S accepts. One pair d b is finished.

21
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:09,203
R on m. 9 goes to 10. Close the same way through 6. The set is 2, 3, 6, 7, 8, 10. Call it T. It accepts.

22
00:03:09,653 --> 00:03:19,935
S on d. Only 3 has a d edge, to 4. That is Q again. S on f gives R again. T works the same way. No new sets appear.

23
00:03:20,385 --> 00:03:27,169
All other entries go to X. For example, Q on d is X: after d, only b is allowed.

24
00:03:27,619 --> 00:03:35,150
Here is the full table. P, S and T accept. Every row has one entry for each of the four letters.

25
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:49,360
Here is the D F A. P goes to Q on d and to R on f. Q goes to S on b, and R goes to T on m. From S and from T, d goes back to Q, and f goes back to R.

26
00:03:49,810 --> 00:03:59,239
There are many arrows into X, so we do not draw them. Every arrow that is not drawn goes to X. The notes draw the same machine.

27
00:03:59,689 --> 00:04:11,060
Step 3. The notes call the bubbles G, H and X. G holds the accepting states P, S and T. H holds Q and R. X is the error state.

28
00:04:11,510 --> 00:04:20,427
Write the target bubble for each state and letter. P, S and T all go to H on d and f, and to X on b and m.

29
00:04:20,877 --> 00:04:31,949
Inside H, Q and R differ. On b, Q goes to G, but R goes to X. On m, it is the other way round. This is an ambiguous transition.

30
00:04:32,399 --> 00:04:43,343
So H splits into H1, which is Q, and H2, which is R. Round 2 changes nothing. The minimal D F A has G, H1, H2 and X.

31
00:04:43,793 --> 00:04:57,511
Here is the minimal D F A, as the notes draw it after the split. G is the start, and the only accepting state. H1 waits for b. H2 waits for m. All other letters go to X.

32
00:04:57,961 --> 00:05:04,553
Test d b f m. G, H1, G, H2, G. It ends in G, so accept.

33
00:05:05,003 --> 00:05:12,277
Test d b f. The run stops in H2, which does not accept. The pair f m is not finished.

34
00:05:12,727 --> 00:05:16,375
Test b d. G goes to X on b. Reject.

35
00:05:16,825 --> 00:05:27,108
Six states became four. A python check ran the N F A, both D F As and the regex on every string up to length 7. They agree.

36
00:05:27,558 --> 00:05:40,785
Now the first question in questions-pre 4.8. Produce an N F A for x, y star, z or c w, a, b optional. Then convert it to a D F A and minimize it.

37
00:05:41,235 --> 00:05:50,237
This question has no posted solution. So this is our solution, and a python check confirms each step. Pause the video and try it.

38
00:05:50,687 --> 00:05:59,647
Here is our N F A, built with the templates. Concatenation joins an exit to the next entry. The letter edges are in blue.

39
00:06:00,097 --> 00:06:07,415
y star uses the repetition template: 2 in, 4 back to 3, 4 out, and 2 to 5 to skip.

40
00:06:07,865 --> 00:06:15,267
z or c w uses the alternation template: 5 is the entry and 11 is the exit.

41
00:06:15,717 --> 00:06:32,400
b optional is b or epsilon, slide 04a page 45. So it is an alternation of the b machine and the epsilon machine, 15 to 16. 17 is the only accepting state. 17 states.

42
00:06:32,850 --> 00:06:48,786
The start state A is the closure of 1. State 1 has no epsilon edges, so A is just 1. A on x goes to 2. Close: 2 reaches 3 and 5, and 5 reaches 6 and 8. B is 2, 3, 5, 6, 8.

43
00:06:49,236 --> 00:06:59,177
B on y. 3 goes to 4. Close: 4 reaches 3 and 5, and 5 reaches 6 and 8. C is 3, 4, 5, 6, 8.

44
00:06:59,627 --> 00:07:05,494
B on z. 6 goes to 7, and 7 reaches 11. D is 7, 11.

45
00:07:05,944 --> 00:07:13,304
B on c. 8 goes to 9. E is the set with only 9. The other letters from A and B go to X.

46
00:07:13,754 --> 00:07:22,181
C has the same y, z and c edges as B. So C on y is C, C on z is D, and C on c is E.

47
00:07:22,631 --> 00:07:41,105
D on a. 11 goes to 12. Close: 12 reaches 13 and 15, 15 reaches 16, and 16 reaches 17. F is 12, 13, 15, 16, 17. It contains 17, so F accepts. The b is optional.

48
00:07:41,555 --> 00:07:51,433
E on w. 9 goes to 10, and 10 reaches 11. G is 10, 11. G on a goes to 12, so G on a is F again.

49
00:07:51,883 --> 00:08:07,051
F on b. 13 goes to 14, and 14 reaches 17. H is 14, 17. It accepts. H has no letter edges, so every letter from H goes to X. Nine states with X.

50
00:08:07,501 --> 00:08:16,418
Here is the table: nine states, seven letters. Most entries are X, because each state allows only one or a few letters.

51
00:08:16,868 --> 00:08:31,908
Here is the D F A without the X arrows. B and C look alike: both go to C on y, to D on z, and to E on c. D and G also look alike: both go to F on a. Minimization will decide.

52
00:08:32,358 --> 00:08:45,073
Begin with three bubbles. F and H accept. X is the error state. All the other states are in one bubble, called rest. Each row shows the target bubble for each of the seven letters.

53
00:08:45,523 --> 00:09:02,760
F and H differ on b: F goes back into F H, and H goes to X. So they split. In the rest bubble, the rows fall into four groups. A reads x. B and C read y, z and c. D and G read a. E reads w.

54
00:09:03,210 --> 00:09:15,093
Round 2. B and C go to the same bubbles: B C on y, D G on z, and E on c. D and G both go to F on a. Nothing splits, so we stop.

55
00:09:15,543 --> 00:09:20,705
Nine states became seven: A, B C, D G, E, F, H, and X.

56
00:09:21,155 --> 00:09:32,761
Here is the minimal D F A. It follows the regex from left to right. x, then a loop on y, then z, or c and w, then a, then an optional b.

57
00:09:33,211 --> 00:09:40,592
Test x y y c w a b. A, B C, B C, B C, E, D G, F, H. Accept.

58
00:09:41,042 --> 00:09:46,759
Test x z. The run ends in D G, which does not accept. The a is missing.

59
00:09:47,209 --> 00:09:55,657
A python check ran all the machines and the regex on every string over the seven letters, up to length 6. They agree.

60
00:09:56,107 --> 00:10:05,238
The second question. Produce an N F A for r m b or c, optional, then n s f star. Then convert and minimize.

61
00:10:05,688 --> 00:10:11,640
Again there is no posted solution. This is our solution. Pause the video and try it.

62
00:10:12,090 --> 00:10:17,445
Here is our N F A. The letter edges are r m b, c, and n s f.

63
00:10:17,895 --> 00:10:23,292
The inner alternation, r m b or c, has entry 2 and exit 11.

64
00:10:23,742 --> 00:10:36,094
The question mark means: this or epsilon. So a second alternation, with entry 1 and exit 12, picks either the inner machine or the epsilon machine from 9 to 10.

65
00:10:36,544 --> 00:10:51,477
Then n s f star uses the repetition template: 12 in to 13, 16 back to 13, 16 out to 17, and 12 to 17 to skip. 17 is the only accepting state.

66
00:10:51,927 --> 00:11:11,106
The start state A. From 1 we reach 2 and 9. From 2 we reach 3 and 7. From 9 we reach 10, then 12, then 13 and 17. A is 1, 2, 3, 7, 9, 10, 12, 13, 17. It contains 17, so A accepts the empty string.

67
00:11:11,556 --> 00:11:16,036
A on r. 3 goes to 4. B is the set with only 4.

68
00:11:16,486 --> 00:11:28,667
A on c. 7 goes to 8. Close: 8 reaches 11, then 12, then 13 and 17. C is 8, 11, 12, 13, 17. It accepts.

69
00:11:29,117 --> 00:11:39,869
A on n. 13 goes to 14. D is the set with only 14. The optional part was skipped. A on m, b, s or f goes to X.

70
00:11:40,319 --> 00:11:44,714
B on m. 4 goes to 5. E is the set with only 5.

71
00:11:45,164 --> 00:11:56,983
E on b. 5 goes to 6. Close: 6 reaches 11, 12, 13 and 17. F is 6, 11, 12, 13, 17. It accepts.

72
00:11:57,433 --> 00:12:02,958
D on s. 14 goes to 15. G is the set with only 15.

73
00:12:03,408 --> 00:12:14,117
G on f. 15 goes to 16. Close: 16 reaches 13 and 17. H is 13, 16, 17. It accepts.

74
00:12:14,567 --> 00:12:23,741
C, F and H all contain 13. So each one goes to D on n. All other entries go to X. Nine states with X.

75
00:12:24,191 --> 00:12:29,908
Here is the table. Nine states and seven letters. A, C, F and H accept.

76
00:12:30,358 --> 00:12:37,867
Here is the D F A without the X arrows. C, F and H look alike: each one only goes to D on n.

77
00:12:38,317 --> 00:12:44,995
Three bubbles again. A C F H accept. B D E G do not. X is the error state.

78
00:12:45,445 --> 00:13:00,613
A differs from C, F and H: A also reads r and c. In the other bubble, each state reads a different letter: B reads m, D reads s, E reads b, and G reads f. So that bubble splits into four.

79
00:13:01,063 --> 00:13:09,148
Round 2. C, F and H have equal rows: n goes to D, and every other letter goes to X. Nothing splits.

80
00:13:09,598 --> 00:13:14,697
Nine states became seven: A, B, E, C F H, D, G, and X.

81
00:13:15,147 --> 00:13:26,219
Here is the minimal D F A. From A, the path r m b or the letter c leads to C F H. The letter n leads straight into the loop n s f.

82
00:13:26,669 --> 00:13:33,346
Test r m b n s f. A, B, E, C F H, D, G, C F H. Accept.

83
00:13:33,796 --> 00:13:42,628
Test n s f c. The loop ends in C F H, and c goes to X. The optional part must come first. Reject.

84
00:13:43,078 --> 00:13:49,798
A python check ran all the machines and the regex on every string up to length 6. They agree.

85
00:13:50,248 --> 00:14:00,211
To summarize. An optional part, x question mark, is x or epsilon. Build it with the alternation template and the epsilon machine.

86
00:14:00,661 --> 00:14:09,087
With a large alphabet, most arrows go to the error state. Draw X once, and say that every missing arrow goes to X.

87
00:14:09,537 --> 00:14:18,519
Minimize with X in the machine. States with equal rows stay together. Here: B and C, D and G, and C, F, H.

88
00:14:18,969 --> 00:14:26,862
The state counts were 10, 6, 4 for Under Pressure, and 17, 9, 7 for both questions in 4.8.

89
00:14:27,312 --> 00:14:39,941
Always test some accepted and some rejected strings on every machine and on the regex. The next video is a hard design question: binary numbers divisible by 5.
